# Rust production deployment log

All timestamps UTC. User authorized an in-place Rust deployment attempt on 2026-09-13. No CloudFormation update, instance replacement, volume deletion, instance upgrade or continuous Bedrock configuration is authorized.

## Temporary login behavior

The first login cache file was written 13:12:09Z with access-token expiry 13:27:09Z. Profile `cds-login` correctly points to the existing login session; its SHA-256 matches the cache filename; owner/mode are correct; DPoP key and refresh token are present; no `AWS_LOGIN_CACHE_DIRECTORY` or `~/.aws/credentials` override exists; UTC clock was correct. A command at 13:25 received `CreateOAuth2Token INVALID_REQUEST` during proactive refresh. Pending alternate login commands were stopped before authorization, without logout.

The user's refreshed cache was written 13:29:15Z with access expiry 13:44:15Z. Serialized calls succeeded until the app's 119th Titan vector, then refresh failed at 13:39:18Z—inside the CLI's five-minute proactive-refresh window—with the same invalid-grant response. No concurrent AWS process was running, and the cache mtime did not advance. A refresh race is therefore unlikely. AWS documents 15-minute access credentials that should refresh for up to 12 hours ([CLI guide](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/cli-configure-sign-in.html)); this behavior is not expected expiry. Open AWS CLI issue [#10330](https://github.com/aws/aws-cli/issues/10330) reports the same refresh-token failure family through 2026-09-11 on CLI 2.36.43 with synchronized WSL time. Closed P0 service-API issue [#10267](https://github.com/aws/aws-cli/issues/10267) records the exact `INVALID_REQUEST` invalid-grant response and an alternate authentication region as its temporary workaround. An AWS Sign-In refresh regression is the leading diagnosis, not proved. At the next human-attended test, run `/usr/local/bin/aws login --profile cds-login --region us-east-1`; confirm the displayed authorization host starts with `us-east-1.signin.aws.amazon.com`, then make one serial unpaid STS call after the 15-minute refresh boundary. This issuer-region change is a test derived from issue #10267, not an established fix for our refresh flow. No token or credential value was printed or saved here.

